From fbb848848e598412f6745d9dc13f681335e20362 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:10:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(verify): the hidden backstop convicts at any height
 the band sweeps, not one plane

The mid-turn overhang backstop read each ask at the lowest plane of its
cells, on the claim that a lower plane only adds convictions. That holds
for the column law, not for the hidden law: hidden_at(h) is the shadow
of what hangs over h less the section at h, and the section comes and
goes. Stock hidden in a cavity over a floor is part material at the
floor's plane, so a band that crossed it higher up passed (#1324 safety
review: A 0 -> 2 degrees on a plunge Z 0 -> -40, read at Z -20.37).

Now every column the backstop asks is read over the whole range of
heights the band's bottom takes there:

- Cell::heights_over gives the closed range per cell and stretch (lowest
  bottom to the highest any pose of the cell holds it).
- hidden_span.rs answers a range exactly in Z: it walks the vertical
  through the point down the model's facets counting depth; a stretch at
  depth 0 under a crossing is what the plane law calls hidden. Stock top
  and the lowest earlier clearing cut cap it. An unreadable vertical
  convicts.
- hidden_in_cover keeps the lowest plane's region reading and adds the
  range reading over the probes, the plane's samples and a 0.5 mm
  lattice clipped to where part material hangs over the box.

No new slices: the plane budget stays at 20 per band per move.

Pins: a_cavity_the_band_passes_on_the_way_down_is_convicted_at_any_height
(the review's counter-probe plus thin cavities all down the sweep),
stock_hidden_in_a_cavity_above_the_asks_lowest_plane_is_convicted (real
geometry) with its clean twin, and hidden_span's unit tests.

Refs #1316

